      ],
      "statement": "There exists a family of real probability laws lambda_N, for every natural N, equal to the actual pushforward of normalized counting measure on all Fin N -> Bool configurations by the signed sum divided by sqrt(N). The true sign is +1 and the false sign is -1. Its zero law is dirac0, and the successor sequence lambda_(N+1) converges weakly to gaussianReal0,1 (mean0, variance1). No law, normalization, moment, independence or CLT certificate is supplied.",
      "formula": "\\mu_N=2^{-N}\\sum_{\\varepsilon\\in\\{0,1\\}^N}\\delta_\\varepsilon,\\quad S_N(\\varepsilon)=N^{-1/2}\\sum_{j=1}^N\\sigma(\\varepsilon_j),\\quad\\lambda_N=(S_N)_\\#\\mu_N,\\quad\\lambda_0=\\delta_0,\\quad\\lambda_{N+1}\\Rightarrow\\mathcal N(0,1).",
      "steps": [
        {
          "title": "Build the actual laws, including zero",
          "text": "The finite carrier has positive cardinal2^N. Scaling counting measure by its reciprocal gives mass1. All finite functions are measurable and Bochner integrable; each normalized sum is pushed forward to a genuine probability law. AtN0 the empty sum is0, so its map law is the unit atom at0, without any fallback.",
          "formula": "\\mu_N(\\{0,1\\}^N)=2^{-N}2^N=1,\\qquad S_0=0,\\quad\\lambda_0=\\delta_0.",
          "lean": "private countLaw_probability; countLaw_integral; actualLaw; actualLaw_zero"
        },
        {
          "title": "Produce the single-coordinate moments",
          "text": "Use the actual balanced Bool count law and sigma(true)=1, sigma(false)=-1. The finite integral is the two-value average; hence the coordinate has mean0 and secondmoment1. These moments and measurable sign map are proof ingredients, never target premises.",
          "formula": "\\mathbb E\\sigma=\\tfrac12(1-1)=0,\\qquad\\mathbb E\\sigma^2=\\tfrac12(1+1)=1.",
          "lean": "private sign_mean; sign_second_moment; measurable_of_countable"
        },
        {
          "title": "Prove the finite characteristic-function product",
          "text": "For every configuration, exp(i t sum sigma_j) is the product of exp(i t sigma_j). Sum over all configurations using Fintype.sum_pow; multiplying by2^-N gives the Nth power of the actual single-coordinate characteristic function. This is an authored count-law adapter, not an assumed independence or copied infinite-iid construction.",
          "formula": "\\widehat\\lambda_N(t)=\\left[\\tfrac12\\sum_{b\\in\\{0,1\\}}\\exp(i t\\sigma(b)/\\sqrt N)\\right]^N.",
          "lean": "private coordinate_charFun; unscaled_charFun; normalized_charFun; Complex.exp_sum; Fintype.sum_pow"
        },
        {
          "title": "Apply the pinned scalar CLT to the derived moments",
          "text": "Mathlib characteristic-function CLT applies to this actual measurable balanced coordinate with its internally derived mean0 and secondmoment1. It yields convergence of the same Nth-power expression to exp(-t^2/2), the exact characteristic function of mean0 variance1 Gaussian.",
          "formula": "\\widehat\\lambda_N(t)\\longrightarrow\\exp(-t^2/2)=\\widehat{\\mathcal N(0,1)}(t).",
